    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **James Wood** (76.28) and **Austin Riley** (73.19) lead the way as our strongest plays, both capitalizing on favorable park factors and the aggressive baserunning profiles that define this roster. **James Wood** benefits from a premium leadoff spot and a soft matchup against Rodriguez, while **Austin Riley** sees his score inflated by a significant park boost and a high probability of exploiting the slow delivery of Rogers, which is a major signal for our league\u2019s priority on stolen bases. **Brice Turang** (72.70) remains an auto-start; his matchup against a high-xERA arm like Sugano makes him the most reliable source of production outside our top tier. We are banking on this trio to anchor our counting stats while the rest of the lineup provides steady output against mid-rotation arms.\n\nThe closest call on the bench was **Mickey Moniak**, whose score of 42.03 hovered just above the marginal threshold but ultimately failed to displace the efficiency of our active core. While he provides decent utility, the platoon disadvantage against Drohan capped his ceiling, and we preferred the matchup upside of **Zach McKinstry** despite the lower raw projection. We are monitoring **Edwin Arroyo** closely, as he remains tethered to the bench with a 40.42 score, though he is currently flagged with a 41% rain risk that further validates keeping him sidelined. \n\nRisks are concentrated around our lower-scoring hitters facing elite arms. **Seiya Suzuki** (46.11) and **Gabriel Moreno** (47.56) are navigating tough matchups against Jones and Palmquist, respectively; while they are in the lineup, they are essentially playing to maintain our floor. **Corbin Carroll** (60.09) is a vital start despite the matchup, as his elite speed profile provides a \"hidden\" points floor even if his contact quality is suppressed by Palmquist. We have successfully avoided starting **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **Travis Bazzana**, both of whom dipped below our 40-point efficiency floor due to brutal individual matchups against high-end talent like Skubal and McClanahan.",
